<exercise xml:id="exercise-vectors-create-lin-comb">
	<title>Creating a linear combination</title>

	<statement><p>
		Determine values for scalars <m> k_1, k_2, k_3 </m> so that the vector equality is true.
		<md> k_1 (4,-1,-1) + k_2 (-3,1,2) + k_3 (-2,-1,-6) = (1,2,10) </md>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		First compute
		<md>
			k_1 (4,-1,-1) + k_2 (-3,1,2) + k_3 (-2,-1,-6)
			= ( 4 k_1 - 3 k_2 - 2 k_3, - k_1 + k_2 - k_3, - k_1 + 2 k_2 - 6 k_3 )
		</md>.
		Thus our vector equality becomes
		<md> ( 4 k_1 - 3 k_2 - 2 k_3, - k_1 + k_2 - k_3, - k_1 + 2 k_2 - 6 k_3 ) = (1,2,10) </md>.
		Matching corresponding components on each side of this equality leads to a linear system of equations.
		<md>
			\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
				4 k_1 \amp - \amp 3 k_2 \amp - \amp 2 k_3 \amp = \amp  1 \\
				- k_1 \amp + \amp   k_2 \amp - \amp   k_3 \amp = \amp  2 \\
				- k_1 \amp + \amp 2 k_2 \amp - \amp 6 k_3 \amp = \amp 10
			\end{sysofeqns}
		</md>
		Convert the system into an augmented matrix and reduce.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				 4 \amp -3 \amp -2 \amp  1 \\
				-1 \amp  1 \amp -1 \amp  2 \\
				-1 \amp  2 \amp -6 \amp 10
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad\rowredarrow\qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  2 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Thus the original vector equality is true for scalar values
		<m> k_1 = 2 </m>, <m> k_2 = 3 </m>, <m> k_3 = -1 </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

